<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Owari Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Owari Province was an old province of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, corresponding to the western part of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>. It was the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, who united it by 1559, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> it formed the Owari domain, the senior of the three Tokugawa branch houses, ruled from Nagoya Castle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/owari-province.jpg" alt="Owari Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Owari.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Owari Province lay in the area that is now the western half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a>, including the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>. It was created in 646 and bordered Mikawa, Mino and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-province">Ise Provinces</a>. The Sakai River, whose name means border river, separated Owari from Mino. Its abbreviated name was Bishū, and it was one of the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a>. Under the Engishiki classification it was ranked a superior country and a near country, by its distance from the capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been settled since at least the Japanese Paleolithic period, and Owari is mentioned in records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period, including the Kujiki. Early records speak of a powerful Owari clan, related to or allied with the Yamato clan, which built large kofun burial mounds in several places in the province; bronze objects and mirrors dating from the 4th century have been recovered from them. </p><p>Atsuta Shrine, of very ancient origin, ranks with Ise Shrine in importance and holds one of the Imperial Regalia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, the sword Kusanagi-no-Tsurugi. The Engishiki divided the province into eight counties, which lasted as administrative units into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The provincial capital is traditionally placed in what is now Inazawa, while the Ichinomiya stood in what is now Ichinomiya.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a> the province was divided into many shōen controlled by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> clans, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> it had split into small territories largely dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> reunified the province and began his campaign to reunify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from Kiyosu Castle. Many of his retainers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa">Katō Kiyomasa</a>, were natives of Owari and later became daimyōs under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> assigned the province as a domain to his ninth son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinao">Tokugawa Yoshinao</a>, with official revenues of 619,500 koku, the largest domain in the Tokugawa holdings outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> itself. Yoshinao founded the Owari Tokugawa clan, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gosanke">Gosanke</a>, which held the hereditary right of succession to the position of shōgun if the main line failed. The castle town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a> prospered, and the province was known for its ceramics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the han system was abolished in 1871 the former Owari Domain and Inuyama Domain became short-lived prefectures. They were joined with Nukata Prefecture, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa Province</a>, in January 1872 to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a>. The province continued to exist for some purposes: Owari is named in treaties of 1894 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and between Japan and the United Kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Owari Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oda Nobunaga inherited part of <b>Owari</b> in 1551 and had united the province by 1559.</blockquote><a href="/state/oda-regime">Oda regime</a></li><li><blockquote>Tokugawa Ieyasu gave <b>Owari</b> to his ninth son Tokugawa Yoshinao in 1607.</blockquote><a href="/state/owari-domain">Owari Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Dosan governed by force and intrigue and married his daughter to Oda Nobunaga to secure peace with <b>Owari</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/saito-clan-domain">Saito clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
